WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:04.950
The Joker gives actors the chance to chew
scenery like no other character, but his true

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:08.320
appeal is how much of his backstory remains
shrouded in mystery.

00:00:08.530 --> 00:00:12.700
The only things crazier than the Joker are
the fan theories his cinematic escapades have

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:13.200
inspired.

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:17.960
Here's a fan theory that has been making the
rounds lately: Joaquin Phoenix isn't playing

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:19.270
the Joker in Joker.

00:00:19.270 --> 00:00:21.200
At least, not the Joker we all know and love.

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:24.190
For starters, Phoenix is playing a character
named Arthur Fleck.

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:28.640
We've heard Joker's name as "Jack Napier"
before, most notably in Tim Burton's Batman.

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:29.480
"Jack?

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:32.880
Jack is dead, my friend.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:36.840
You can call me….JOKER."

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:42.000
But he's never had a 100% canonical identity,
and Arthur Fleck is a new one.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:43.460
Frankly, it sounds like a red herring.

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:47.530
Mr. Fleck is also several decades older than
the boy who would become Batman..

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:52.410
This has caused fans to speculate that Phoenix's
Arthur Fleck is not the real Joker, but his

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:56.680
clownish criminal predecessor who inspires
the true Joker years later.

00:00:56.890 --> 00:01:01.980
Joaquin Phoenix's Joker isn't part of the
DCEU and is operating as more of an Elseworlds

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:04.960
tale, which honestly means this could go any
number of ways.

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:09.740
Maybe Batman won't actually be battling his
arch-nemesis until the Joker is in his seventies?

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:14.050
Or perhaps Gotham is such a wacky place that
the psychopathic criminal clown is immortal

00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:15.840
- which has been hinted at in the comics.

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:17.000
Sounds crazy, right?

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:18.360
Well, that's the Joker for you!

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:28.320
Just take a look at some of these other fan
theories about the Clown Prince of Crime that

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:32.980
prove one thing: No matter how insane the
Joker is on the screen, the fans can get even

00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:33.700
insaner.

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:38.540
Most fan theories about the Joker center on
Heath Ledger's performance in The Dark Knight.

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:40.260
This is no surprise, for a few reasons.

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:44.890
Ledger's brilliant work was graced with Oscar
gold, The Dark Knight was a milestone event

00:01:44.890 --> 00:01:49.750
in American popular culture, and Christopher
Nolan's ambiguity about Joker's origins invites

00:01:49.750 --> 00:01:51.240
fans to fill in the blanks.

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:53.720
"You wanna know how I got these scars?"

00:01:55.800 --> 00:02:00.260
Because The Dark Knight can be seen as a thinly-veiled
commentary on the War on Terror, one of the

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:04.920
most popular theories is that the Joker was
a Special Forces soldier who suffered a mental

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:05.420
breakdown.

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:09.100
Given Nolan's deliberately realistic approach,
this theory has merit.

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:13.140
After all, it's not like this Joker learned
about firearms and explosives just from watching

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:13.920
the Outdoor Channel.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:18.660
But some fans who believe Joker is an ex-soldier
have taken this theory to its logical next

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:20.980
level — what if the Joker has PTSD?

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:25.720
Such a scenario is troubling, to be honest,
but before you dismiss this as pure fan fiction,,

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:30.340
consider Bane's class warfare in The Dark
Knight Rises, released just after the time

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:31.360
of Occupy Wall Street.

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:35.480
Is it really it stretch to say that Nolan
imagined the Joker as a war veteran wreaking

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:37.620
vengeance on the society that left him to
die?

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:41.200
Comedian Patton Oswalt disagrees with the
PTSD idea.

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:45.420
While he's best known for his stand-up comedy
specials and voice work, Oswalt is also a

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:47.740
pretty keen observer of superhero stories.

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:52.240
He is an adherent of the "Joker as Special
Forces soldier" school of fan theorizing,

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:54.240
but has added his own unique twist.

00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:59.040
In a lengthy Facebook post published just
two months shy of the film's 10th anniversary,

00:02:59.040 --> 00:02:59.540
Oswalt wrote:

00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:03.640
"I just re-watched The Dark Knight, and another
wrinkle came to mind about the Joker.

00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:07.380
What if he's not only ex-military, but ex-military
intelligence?

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:09.340
Specifically — interrogation?

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:13.720
He seems to be very good at the kind of mind-f---ery
that sustained, professional interrogation

00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:14.220
requires."

00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:19.660
"Never start with the head… the victim gets
all fuzzy he can’t feel the…

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:20.160
Arrgh!

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:22.780
see?"

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:26.010
Lots of fans seem to like the way Oswalt thinks.

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:28.920
The post has received over 17,000 reactions.

00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:33.720
"Byooo!"

00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:39.340
While Nolan has never answered the fan theorizing
— and likely never will — the "Joker as

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:42.720
a military interrogator" theory has given
fans plenty to mull over.

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:47.980
In a post popular enough to receive some major
media coverage, Reddit user generalzee proposed

00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:50.500
that the Joker was actually the hero of The
Dark Knight.

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:53.140
"Ha ha ha.

00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:54.440
Ho. hee hee.

00:03:57.360 --> 00:03:59.500
And I thought my jokes were bad."

00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:04.500
According to this theory, before Joker showed
up, Gotham was so infected with organized

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:08.830
crime and corrupt officials that the city
turned to a masked vigilante to save them.

00:04:08.830 --> 00:04:10.000
Then, the Redditor writes,

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:14.180
"Along comes the Joker and by the end of a
very short time, almost all organized crime

00:04:14.180 --> 00:04:18.680
was eliminated, many corrupt officials were
imprisoned or dead, and the city's vigilante

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:19.880
even went into hiding.

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:23.319
[...] This was all part of Joker's masterfully
executed plan."

00:04:23.319 --> 00:04:25.889
Well, when you look at it like that, maybe
Joker wasn't so bad.

00:04:25.889 --> 00:04:27.240
"Ahh ta ta ta ta.

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:31.490
Let's not blow this out of proportion."

00:04:31.960 --> 00:04:33.100
But what about the boats?

00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:37.080
Joker was literally tempting prisoners and
pedestrians to blow each other up!

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:40.849
Turns out the Redditor has an answer for that
too, as the Joker, quote, "proved that there

00:04:40.849 --> 00:04:44.389
was good even in the most supposedly despicable
of Gotham's inmates."

00:04:44.389 --> 00:04:46.439
That's a pretty generous reading of his motivations.

00:04:46.439 --> 00:04:50.789
But what is undeniable is that by the end
of The Dark Knight, Gotham's criminal underbelly

00:04:50.789 --> 00:04:51.640
is destroyed.

00:04:51.889 --> 00:04:55.639
All it took was a few sticks of dynamite,
the murder of countless innocents, and a city

00:04:55.640 --> 00:04:57.330
of millions beset by terror.

00:04:57.520 --> 00:04:58.520
Gee, Joker… thanks?

00:04:59.380 --> 00:05:02.860
Another theory that's been making the rounds
is that the Joker was a spy.

00:05:02.860 --> 00:05:03.460
What's the evidence?

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:08.460
For one, Joker's knowledge of Gotham's power
hierarchy demonstrates a background in surveillance.

00:05:08.470 --> 00:05:13.080
There's also the fact that Joker can withstand
— and apply — both physical and psychological

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:13.580
torture.

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:17.999
And there's the fact that, whether you believe
he was a hero or villain, the Joker does want

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:21.180
to "save" Gotham by rooting out the city's
criminality and corruption.

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:22.440
It's an interesting take.

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:25.940
Whether or not you buy it, though, the most
fascinating thing about this theory is that

00:05:25.949 --> 00:05:30.600
it shows just how much fans want Joker to
be the good guy… albeit in his own homicidal,

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:31.360
psychopathic way.

00:05:33.640 --> 00:05:35.080
"I just want my phone call."

00:05:35.980 --> 00:05:39.680
Before Deadpool broke the fourth wall in his
self-titled debut, the Joker did the same

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:42.920
thing in The Dark Knight, at least according
to one popular fan theory.

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:47.460
While Joker breaking the fourth wall has occasionally
happened in the comics, he has never done

00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:49.440
so on the big screen… or has he?

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:53.680
Reddit user someonesfreakingout postulated
that the Joker in The Dark Knight knows he's

00:05:53.689 --> 00:05:54.689
in a movie.

00:05:54.689 --> 00:05:58.680
The Redditor argues that most of Joker's "comedy"
is meant not for the characters in the movie,

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:00.000
but for a hidden audience: us.

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:03.000
"I'm gonna make this pencil disappear"

00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:05.980
"Ta da!”

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:09.280
“it's gone."

00:06:11.380 --> 00:06:15.500
This gives the Joker a characteristic rarely
associated with him, namely sanity.

00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:19.740
You may notice a theme running through these
Joker theories — they all fundamentally

00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:22.930
change the character's arc, and consequently,
the meaning of the movie.

00:06:22.930 --> 00:06:26.520
But what about a theory that doesn't require
any such drastic retconning?

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:31.520
Reddit user DorkKnight52 noticed that the
Joker, a big fan of mass murder, doesn't kill

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:33.780
anyone at Bruce Wayne's party for Harvey Dent.

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:34.520
Why?

00:06:34.540 --> 00:06:38.639
The Joker arrived at Bruce's party with one
goal: to kill Harvey Dent, who he thought

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:39.240
was Batman.

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:43.680
When Batman arrives and dives out the window
to rescue Rachel Dawes, Joker leaves, thinking

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:45.040
Harvey just jumped to his death.

00:06:45.220 --> 00:06:49.300
Later in the film, after Harvey claims to
be Batman, Gotham's District Attorney is carted

00:06:49.300 --> 00:06:51.200
off in a paddy wagon with a police escort.

00:06:51.540 --> 00:06:55.340
We're led to believe this is Batman's plan
to lure out the Joker, but what if it was

00:06:55.340 --> 00:06:57.200
the Joker's plan to lure out Batman?

00:06:57.580 --> 00:07:00.420
If Batman doesn't show, that means he really
is Dent.

00:07:00.620 --> 00:07:04.719
When Batman does arrive, the Joker's master
plan changes — he no longer wants to kill

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:06.600
Gotham's white knight, but corrupt him.

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:12.240
"You live...you die.

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:14.820
MMM, now we're talking."

00:07:16.860 --> 00:07:19.360
What if Jared Leto's Joker was actually Jason
Todd?

00:07:19.640 --> 00:07:23.320
This theory has been dancing in fans' minds
ever since it was first postulated around

00:07:23.340 --> 00:07:26.440
the time Batman v. Superman: Dawn of Justice
was released.

00:07:26.620 --> 00:07:30.840
In the comics, Jason Todd was the post-Dick
Grayson Robin, until he was beaten and blown

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:31.760
up by the Joker.

00:07:31.860 --> 00:07:36.399
The DCEU has indicated that Jason Todd's story
would be part of the cinematic canon, with

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:39.280
the blown-up Boy Wonder's costume appearing
in BvS.

00:07:39.540 --> 00:07:43.860
But when Jared Leto appeared months later
as a much younger Joker in Suicide Squad,

00:07:43.860 --> 00:07:46.500
some fans wondered if this Joker could be
Jason Todd.

00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:51.180
Frankly, while this theory is interesting,
fans probably like it mainly because the alternative

00:07:51.189 --> 00:07:55.900
is so unappealing — Leto's tatted-up interpretation
of the Joker is the Joker.

00:07:55.900 --> 00:07:58.809
Yeah, we'll take the highly implausible, convoluted
theory.

00:07:58.809 --> 00:07:59.600
Speaking of which...

00:08:00.140 --> 00:08:04.500
Technically, this theory isn't from any of
the Batman films, but it is so awesomely out

00:08:04.500 --> 00:08:05.580
there we had to share it.

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:09.059
According to Redditor Bakkidza, Fight Club could be read as an origin

00:08:09.060 --> 00:08:10.060
story for the Joker.

00:08:10.249 --> 00:08:14.180
David Fincher and Christopher Nolan do share
similar aesthetics, so it's interesting to

00:08:14.180 --> 00:08:14.680
consider.

00:08:14.840 --> 00:08:18.840
The theory points out that just like Joker,
Edward Norton's Narrator, who — spoiler

00:08:18.849 --> 00:08:24.129
alert — is also Brad Pitt's Tyler Durden,
has a manic personality, a gang of loyal minions,

00:08:24.129 --> 00:08:26.980
and is an agent of chaos rebelling against
the status quo.

00:08:27.340 --> 00:08:29.300
"You were looking for a way to change your
life.

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:31.300
You cannot do this on your own.

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:34.160
All the ways you wish you could be?"

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:35.540
"That's me."

00:08:35.980 --> 00:08:39.140
Oh, he also shoots out his cheek, giving himself
a messed up face.

00:08:39.140 --> 00:08:39.680
Who knows?

00:08:39.800 --> 00:08:43.680
This may have been Joker's last story about
how he got his scars, if Batman hadn't interrupted

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:44.180
him.

00:08:44.180 --> 00:08:48.340
The appeal underlying this theory is pretty
simple — what could be better than one internet

00:08:48.340 --> 00:08:49.460
fan-favorite film?

00:08:49.460 --> 00:08:50.180
Two, of course!

00:08:50.660 --> 00:08:53.700
"I am Jack's complete lack of surprise.”

00:08:53.840 --> 00:08:54.340
“What?"

00:08:55.060 --> 00:08:58.680
Perhaps the most exciting theory is the one
that has been staring us in the face this

00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:01.360
whole time: Batman and the Joker are brothers!

00:09:01.480 --> 00:09:05.460
According to this theory, Thomas and Martha
Wayne had another child before Bruce that

00:09:05.460 --> 00:09:09.350
was born with a mental disorder and was confined
to a psychiatric hospital.

00:09:09.350 --> 00:09:09.960
That child?

00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:12.060
Thomas Wayne Jr., a.k.a. the Joker!

00:09:12.450 --> 00:09:16.040
The comics have danced with this idea for
a while now, but fans have argued there are

00:09:16.040 --> 00:09:17.660
some hints dropped in The Dark Knight, too.

00:09:17.660 --> 00:09:22.040
Consider the famous interrogation scene, in
which Joker goes to great lengths to explain

00:09:22.040 --> 00:09:23.900
to Batman how he is a "freak" like him.

00:09:23.960 --> 00:09:27.780
This scene establishes the two as symbolic
brothers, but what if it's hinting that they're

00:09:27.780 --> 00:09:29.280
literal brothers as well?

00:09:29.540 --> 00:09:33.400
"Brothers don't shake hands, brothers gotta
hug,"

00:09:36.880 --> 00:09:40.640
Joker also says that when the people of Gotham
don't need Batman anymore, they'll cast him

00:09:40.640 --> 00:09:41.140
out.

00:09:41.180 --> 00:09:42.580
"Like a leper!"

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:46.900
Physically and psychologically engaging in
a sadistic game with his own brother?

00:09:46.900 --> 00:09:48.060
That's right up the Joker's alley.

00:09:48.420 --> 00:09:51.520
This theory doesn't concern one Batman movie,
but several of them.

00:09:51.800 --> 00:09:55.490
The idea is simple: as soon as one Joker is
gone, he's replaced by another.

00:09:55.490 --> 00:10:00.550
If we're being literal, this is the only "theory"
that is 100% true — from a business perspective,

00:10:00.550 --> 00:10:05.360
Warner Bros. does more or less need a new
Joker in every incarnation of the Batman franchise.

00:10:05.460 --> 00:10:07.560
He may be crazy, but the dude is money.

00:10:07.710 --> 00:10:12.300
While the Joker is necessary from a financial
and entertainment perspective in our universe,

00:10:12.300 --> 00:10:17.091
what if, in some strange meta-textual way,
he was also necessary because the DC Universe

00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:18.560
itself needs a Joker?

00:10:21.300 --> 00:10:25.460
Redditor Gatta Post Fast argues this while
trying to create a correlation between the

00:10:25.460 --> 00:10:28.760
Tim Burton and Joel Schumacher Bat-verse and
Zack Snyder's.

00:10:28.840 --> 00:10:32.970
According to this theory, when Jack Nicholson's
Joker died, he was eventually replaced by

00:10:32.970 --> 00:10:33.740
Jared Leto's.

00:10:33.970 --> 00:10:38.340
Thus, the Burton-Schumacher Batman is the
same one as the DCEU Batfleck.

00:10:38.340 --> 00:10:42.060
Vague sequels and "soft reboots" are increasingly
the norm in Hollywood.

00:10:42.220 --> 00:10:46.490
And composer Danny Elfman did score films
in both franchises, bringing back the Batman

00:10:46.490 --> 00:10:47.750
march for Justice League.

00:10:47.750 --> 00:10:51.680
Could this be a hint that the DC films all
exist in one giant, shared universe?

00:10:51.680 --> 00:10:52.680
Eh...

00:10:52.840 --> 00:10:54.880
"I find that unlikely."

00:10:55.140 --> 00:10:59.680
Still, this kind of theory could play into
the older version of the Joker that Joaquin

00:10:59.680 --> 00:11:00.680
Phoenix is stepping into.

00:11:00.680 --> 00:11:01.280
So who knows?

00:11:01.420 --> 00:11:03.280
Anything is possible in the Batverse!

00:11:03.800 --> 00:11:05.690
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:11:05.690 --> 00:11:09.140
Plus, even more Looper videos about your favorite
movies are coming soon.

00:11:09.280 --> 00:11:12.660
Subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit the
bell so you don't miss a single one.

